  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered to numerous severe thunderstorms are \nexpected this afternoon through the evening hours today. \n\nWe are in standby mode for now as the copious amount of\nenergy/lift/strong upper level diffluent flow (associated with \nthe left exit region of a 100+ KT jet) has produced an \nexpansive area of weak convection embedded in light to moderate \nrain. All of this thick/debris cloud cover will prevent deep\nconvection through the early afternoon, but pockets of fairly\nquick destabilization via breaks of sun should spread into\nwestern PA and remain over our far SE zones to trigger some\ndeeper convection by or shortly after 18Z.  \n\nWe'll be closely monitoring satellite trends and meso-B \nstability and shear fields to better hone in on areas under the\ngun for potential impact of fast-moving TSRA. \n\nPrevious Disc...\n\nThunderstorms are expected to develop ahead of an eastward- \nmoving cold front across the Ohio Valley and along a pre-\nfrontal trough crossing the CWA between roughly 2-9PM. No \nchanges were made to the level 3/5 enhanced risk area from SPC\nfor day 1 with swaths of damaging wind gusts remaining the \nprimary hazard. Diurnal heating within cloud breaks, combined \nwith upper 60s to lower 70s dewpoints, will support moderate \nsurface-based instability across the broad pre-frontal warm \nsector. The most favorable overlap of this instability and 40-50\nkt of effective shear is expected to foster a mix of organized \nclusters and supercells that should evolve and spread eastward \nthrough the day. Severe hail and a few tornadoes will also be\npossible with any sustained supercells. The enhanced level 3/5\nrisk favors a higher confidence/coverage in severe storms with a\nfew significant severe storms possible reaching intensity \nlevels (75+ mph wind gusts and/or EF-2 tornado) we may only \nexperience 1x or 2x/year. \n\nWPC has expanded the level 2/4 excessive rainfall outlook to\ncover the majority of the CWA. However, despite plenty of juice\nand high PWATs supportive of locally intense rain rates, \noverall confidence is low/limited by the lack of a hires model \nsignal and relatively fast/progressive storm motions. Opted to \npass on a FF watch for this cycle and allow the dayshift to \nreassess or issue a more short-fused/targeted watch. \n\nRain showers linger tonight into Wednesday particularly \ndownwind of Lake Erie and over the higher elevations, but for\nthe most part we anticipate a multi-day drying trend with near\nto slightly below normal temps and comfortably low humidity from\nmidweek into the last weekend of July.",
